# Kineto rules for coding agents (v0.11.0)

Kineto (`@dong-gri/kineto`) is a vanilla-first motion/effects library: 54 modules activated by `data-kt-*` attributes or `Kineto.create()`, with React/Vue/jQuery adapters. Use these rules whenever you generate UI with any component library (shadcn/ui, Bootstrap, MUI, Mantine, Chakra, Ant Design, daisyUI, Nuxt UI, PrimeVue, Vuetify) or from a Figma frame.

## Principles

- Kineto adds motion and media effects to markup you already own; it does not replace the component library's behaviour (dialogs, menus, forms, routing).
- Prefer the library's own component when it already provides the behaviour (toast, tooltip, tabs, accordion, dialog, carousel). Use Kineto for what the library does not ship: entrance motion, text motion, counters, image loading effects, hover/pointer effects, marquee, page reveal, cursor, scroll storytelling.
- Never put two things that animate the same property on one element: a Tailwind/tw-animate-css enter animation plus a Kineto reveal, a Bootstrap carousel plus a Kineto slider, two Kineto modules that both write transform (see docs/rfc/module-composition.md).
- Attach with `data-kt-*` attributes on the element that reaches the DOM, call `Kineto.observe()` once at startup so markup added later (framework renders, portals, modals) is picked up and cleaned up, and reach for the React/Vue adapters only when you need the instance (replay, pause, presence).
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: Kineto already renders the meaningful end state for every module, so do not add a second reduced-motion branch around it.
- Read the exact option names from kineto.features.json (or `npx @dong-gri/kineto-mcp`); do not invent options.

## Setup (once per app)

```js
import '@dong-gri/kineto/style.css';
import Kineto from '@dong-gri/kineto';
Kineto.observe(); // scans now and follows later DOM changes
```

React: mount a client `KinetoProvider` that calls `Kineto.observe()` in an effect (shadcn registry: `npx shadcn@latest add @kineto/provider`). Vue/Nuxt: a client plugin that calls `Kineto.observe()`. Static HTML: the CDN snippet with `Kineto.observe()`.

## Attachment grammar

- Activation: `data-kt-<module-kebab>="<variant>"` (omit the value for the default variant). Options: `data-kt-<option-kebab>="value"`; booleans as bare attributes; arrays/objects as JSON strings.
- JavaScript: `Kineto.create('<module>', element, { <variantKey>: '<variant>', ...options })`. React: `useKineto('<module>', options)` / `<Motion as="h1" type="<module>" options={...}>`. Vue: `v-motion="{ type, options }"` / `useKineto()`.
- Component libraries forward unknown props/attrs to their root DOM node, so `data-kt-*` on `<Card>`, `<Button>`, `<UCard>`, `<v-card>` … reaches the DOM. If a component does not forward attributes, wrap it in a plain element or use a ref.
- One transform-writing module per element (tilt, gesture, magnetic, scrollVelocity, parallax, flip). One reveal per section, staggered, rather than one per item.

